APX.ch = {"country":{"name":"Switzerland","tagline":"Terraced lakeshores and Alpine valleys where Chasselas, Petite Arvine and Cornalin grow almost nowhere else, and the Swiss drink nearly all of it.","classification":"Appellations are cantonal. Each canton defines its AOC or AOCs, permitted grapes, yields and minimum sugar levels; federal law sets the framework. Most cantons have a single cantonal AOC (Genève, Valais, Neuchâtel, Ticino, Zürich, Schaffhausen, Graubünden and so on); Vaud divides itself into eight regional AOCs (Chablais, Lavaux, Calamin, Dézaley, La Côte, Côtes de l'Orbe, Bonvillars and Vully), with communal names such as Yvorne, Aigle, Epesses, Saint-Saphorin, Féchy and Mont-sur-Rolle used inside them.\n\nGrand Cru levels exist in several cantons. Vaud has two Grand Cru AOCs (Dézaley and Calamin) plus 'Premier Grand Cru' for individual estates, the Valais allows communes to define Grand Cru rules (Salgesch, Vétroz, Fully, Saint-Léonard and others), Geneva has Premier Cru communes, and the Neuchâtel and Three Lakes cantons have their own. Traditional names are protected locally: Fendant (Valais Chasselas), Dôle (a Valais red blend of Pinot Noir and Gamay), Salvagnin (a Vaud red blend), Oeil de Perdrix (Pinot Noir rosé, especially Neuchâtel), Johannisberg (Sylvaner in the Valais) and Malvoisie (Pinot Gris)."},"regions":[{"name":"Valais","slug":"valais","tagline":"A sun-trap valley of the Rhône with dozens of Alpine grapes and some of Europe's highest vineyards.","ids":["ch-valais"]},{"name":"Vaud","slug":"vaud","tagline":"The terraces of Chasselas above Lake Geneva.","ids":["ch-bonvillars","ch-calamin","ch-chablais","ch-cotes-de-l-orbe","ch-dezaley","ch-la-cote","ch-lavaux","ch-vaud"]},{"name":"Genève","slug":"geneve","tagline":"Switzerland's third canton, a flat crescent of Gamay and Chasselas around the city.","ids":["ch-geneve"]},{"name":"Three Lakes","slug":"three-lakes","tagline":"Pinot Noir, Oeil de Perdrix and Chasselas on Jurassic limestone.","ids":["ch-bern","ch-bielersee","ch-cheyres","ch-jura","ch-neuchatel","ch-vully"]},{"name":"Ticino","slug":"ticino","tagline":"Merlot under the palms, south of the Alps.","ids":["ch-ticino"]},{"name":"German-speaking Switzerland","slug":"german-speaking-switzerland","tagline":"Small lake and valley vineyards, from Basel to the Bündner Herrschaft's Pinot Noir.","ids":["ch-aargau","ch-appenzell-ausserrhoden","ch-appenzell-innerrhoden","ch-basel-landschaft","ch-basel-stadt","ch-glarus","ch-graubunden","ch-luzern","ch-nidwalden","ch-obwalden","ch-schaffhausen","ch-schwyz","ch-solothurn","ch-st-gallen","ch-thunersee","ch-thurgau","ch-uri","ch-zug","ch-zurich","ch-zurichsee"]}],"items":{"ch-aargau":{"name":"Aargau","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":390,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC Aargau has just under 400 hectares of vines on the Jura foothills and along the Aare, Limmat and Reuss, with Pinot Noir and Müller-Thurgau.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-appenzell-ausserrhoden":{"name":"Appenzell Ausserrhoden","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Appenzell Ausserrhoden has a few hectares of vines (about 5 ha together with Innerrhoden), mainly at Lutzenberg and Wienacht above Lake Constance.","terroir":"Molasse slopes facing the lake.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-appenzell-innerrhoden":{"name":"Appenzell Innerrhoden","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Appenzell Innerrhoden has only a tiny vineyard area, in the exclave of Oberegg near Lake Constance. Like most of German-speaking Switzerland, its vines are dominated by Pinot Noir (locally Blauburgunder) and Müller-Thurgau, often still labelled Riesling-Sylvaner.","terroir":"Molasse slopes.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-basel-landschaft":{"name":"Basel-Landschaft","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":114,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau, Chasselas","summary":"Basel-Landschaft has 114 ha of vines in the hills south and east of Basel, at Maisprach, Buus, Arlesheim and Aesch, giving Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and Chasselas.","terroir":"Jura limestone and loess slopes.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-basel-stadt":{"name":"Basel-Stadt","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":5,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Basel-Stadt has only about 5 ha of vines, most famously the Schlipf at Riehen on the German border, a historic slope facing south over the Rhine plain.","terroir":"Limestone and loess.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-bern":{"name":"Bern","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"Three Lakes","area_ha":254,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay","summary":"The canton of Bern has about 254 ha of vines: 221 ha on the north shore of Lake Biel (Bielersee), where villages such as Twann, Ligerz and La Neuveville make Chasselas and Pinot Noir, and 33 ha around Lake Thun and elsewhere. The Bielersee wines are fresh and mineral, from limestone slopes of the Jura foothills.","terroir":"Jura limestone and marl on steep terraces above the lake.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-bielersee":{"name":"Bielersee","cat":"AOC","region":"Three Lakes","created":"AOC (canton of Bern)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir","summary":"The canton of Bern's lake appellation covers terraced vineyards along the north shore of Lake Biel at Ligerz, Twann, Schafis and La Neuveville and the island of St. Petersinsel. Chasselas and Pinot Noir grow on limestone below the Jura; the wine villages are among the prettiest in Switzerland. Bern also has a tiny vineyard on Lake Thun (AOC Thunersee).","terroir":"Jurassic limestone on steep terraces above the lake.","producers":["Andrey Weinbau","Johanniterkeller Twann","Domaine Martin Hubacher"],"rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-bonvillars":{"name":"Bonvillars","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"A Vaud appellation on the north-west shore of Lake Neuchâtel around Grandson and Concise, where Jurassic limestone gives fresh Chasselas and Pinot Noir.","terroir":"Jurassic limestone at the foot of the Jura.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-calamin":{"name":"Calamin","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","subregion":"Lavaux","created":"Grand Cru AOC","area_ha":16,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"Calamin is the smallest Swiss AOC, about 16 hectares of terraces at Epesses in Lavaux, inside the commune next to Dézaley. Its deeper, clay-limestone soils, which slip slowly downhill, give a Chasselas that is fuller and more earthy than Dézaley.","terroir":"Clay-limestone moraine with landslide movement on steep terraces.","producers":["Domaine Duboux","Domaine Bujard","Domaine Fonjallaz"],"nerd_note":"The name may come from 'calamine', a zinc ore, or a local word for the area's clay soils.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-chablais":{"name":"Chablais","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"AOC Chablais covers the Vaud vineyards of the upper Rhône valley between Lake Geneva and Saint-Maurice, with the communes of Villeneuve, Yvorne, Aigle, Ollon and Bex. Yvorne's vineyards grow on a massive landslide deposit of 1584, and Aigle's terraces surround a medieval castle that houses a wine museum. Chablais Chasselas is fuller and warmer than Lavaux's, with notes of flint and smoke.","terroir":"Scree, landslide debris, limestone and gypsum on the Rhône valley floor and slopes; warmed by the föhn.","producers":["Badoux (Aigle Les Murailles)","Domaine de la Pierre Latine","Clos du Rocher (Obrist)","Henri Badoux"],"nerd_note":"Henri Badoux's Aigle 'Les Murailles' label with its lizard is one of the best-known Swiss wine brands.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-cheyres":{"name":"Cheyres","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Local","region":"Three Lakes","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir","summary":"Cheyres is a small local AOC of the canton of Fribourg on the south shore of Lake Neuchâtel, around the village of Cheyres, giving Chasselas and Pinot Noir. With the Vully, it is one of Fribourg's two wine areas.","terroir":"Molasse and moraine on gentle slopes above the lake.","nerd_note":"Fribourg's vineyards lie on both sides of the language border, in French-speaking Cheyres and bilingual Vully.","rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-cotes-de-l-orbe":{"name":"Côtes de l'Orbe","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"A small Vaud appellation around Orbe and Arnex in the north of the canton, at the foot of the Jura, known for Chasselas, Pinot Noir and Gamay.","terroir":"Limestone and moraine.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-dezaley":{"name":"Dézaley","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","subregion":"Lavaux","created":"Grand Cru AOC","area_ha":54,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"Dézaley is a Grand Cru AOC of about 54 hectares on the steepest terraces of Lavaux, between Epesses and Rivaz, founded by Cistercian monks of Hautcrêt and Montheron in the twelfth century. Its Chasselas is the most powerful and long-lived in Switzerland, smoky and honeyed with age, and the city of Lausanne's Clos des Abbayes and Clos des Moines lie here. Dézaley Rouge is a rare red.","terroir":"Conglomerate (poudingue) and marl on terraces facing due south above the lake.","producers":["Domaine Louis Bovard","Blaise Duboux","Luc Massy","Ville de Lausanne","Domaine Fonjallaz"],"nerd_note":"A well-kept Dézaley can taste fresh after thirty years; the Bovard family has kept a vertical library going back decades.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-geneve":{"name":"Genève","cat":"AOC","region":"Genève","created":"Late 1980s (first Swiss cantonal AOC rules)","area_ha":1340,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Gamay, Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamaret, Chardonnay, Merlot, Sauvignon Blanc","summary":"AOC Genève covers about 1,340 hectares around the city, in the Mandement (Satigny, Dardagny, Russin), between the Arve and the Rhône and between the Arve and the lake. Geneva pioneered cantonal AOC rules in Switzerland and has 22 Premier Cru areas. Gamay is the leading grape, with Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamaret and Garanoir, and the canton has a reputation for modern, well-made wines.","terroir":"Glacial moraine and alluvium on gentle slopes; sunny and relatively warm.","producers":["Domaine des Balisiers","Domaine de la Vigne Blanche","Les Hutins","Cave de Genève","Domaine du Paradis"],"nerd_note":"Satigny is one of the largest wine-growing communes in Switzerland.","rslug":"geneve"},"ch-glarus":{"name":"Glarus","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":2,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Glarus has about 2 ha of vines, one of Switzerland's smallest wine cantons, on sheltered slopes near the Walensee and in the Linth plain.","terroir":"Limestone and moraine.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-graubunden":{"name":"Graubünden","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC Graubünden covers vineyards mostly in the Bündner Herrschaft (Fläsch, Maienfeld, Jenins, Malans) on the upper Rhine, plus the Misox valley (south of the Alps, Merlot). The warm föhn wind ripens Pinot Noir of great elegance, and Malans keeps the ancient white Completer alive.","producers":["Gantenbein","Georg Fromm","Thomas Studach","Donatsch","Annatina Pelizzatti","Weinbau von Tscharner","Schloss Reichenau"],"rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-jura":{"name":"Jura","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"Three Lakes","area_ha":18,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Solaris","summary":"Canton Jura has about 18 ha of vines, a recent revival of viticulture in a canton that had almost none, with plantings in the Ajoie and the Delémont valley, often of fungus-resistant varieties.","terroir":"Jura limestone and marl.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-la-cote":{"name":"La Côte","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"La Côte is Vaud's largest appellation, on gentle slopes between Lausanne and Nyon, with the communes of Morges, Aubonne, Féchy, Mont-sur-Rolle, Rolle, Begnins, Luins and Vinzel. Its Chasselas is light, fresh and easy, while Féchy and Mont-sur-Rolle give more structured examples, and Pinot Noir, Gamay and Gamaret are important reds.","terroir":"Glacial moraine and molasse on broad, gentle slopes facing the lake and the Alps.","producers":["Domaine de la Colombe (Raymond Paccot)","Château de Luins","Domaine Henri Cruchon","Bolle"],"nerd_note":"Raymond Paccot's biodynamic Domaine de la Colombe in Féchy is one of Switzerland's most respected estates.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-lavaux":{"name":"Lavaux","cat":"AOC","region":"Vaud","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":830,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"AOC Lavaux covers the terraced vineyards between Lausanne and Vevey (and up to Montreux), the most spectacular wine landscape in Switzerland and a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2007. About 830 hectares of walls and terraces drop steeply to Lake Geneva in the communes of Lutry, Villette, Epesses, Cully, Saint-Saphorin, Chexbres and Chardonne, around the two Grand Cru enclaves of Dézaley and Calamin. Chasselas is supreme, giving wines of floral delicacy at Lutry and of depth and minerality at Saint-Saphorin and Epesses.","terroir":"Glacial moraine, marl, limestone and conglomerate on terraces with stone walls; the lake reflects light and warmth (the 'three suns').","producers":["Blaise Duboux","Domaine Louis Bovard","Luc Massy","Domaine Bujard","Domaine Croix Duplex","Domaine Testuz","Ville de Lausanne"],"nerd_note":"A thirty-kilometre hiking route through the Lavaux terraces links the villages from Lutry to Chillon.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-luzern":{"name":"Luzern","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":94,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Müller-Thurgau, Pinot Noir, Solaris","summary":"Luzern has 94 ha of vines, a fast-growing small wine canton with vineyards around Lake Lucerne, Lake Sempach and the Seetal, including the historic estate of Schloss Heidegg on Lake Baldegg.","terroir":"Moraine and molasse on lake slopes.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-neuchatel":{"name":"Neuchâtel","cat":"AOC","region":"Three Lakes","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":605,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris","summary":"AOC Neuchâtel covers about 600 hectares on Jurassic limestone along the north-west shore of Lake Neuchâtel, from Le Landeron to Vaumarcus, with Auvernier, Cortaillod and Boudry at its heart. Chasselas and Pinot Noir dominate. Neuchâtel's Oeil de Perdrix, a pale salmon rosé of Pinot Noir, is a local invention, and unfiltered Chasselas released on the third Wednesday of January is a cult.","terroir":"Jurassic limestone and marl, like the Côte d'Or, under the Jura escarpment.","producers":["Domaine de Montmollin","Château d'Auvernier","Domaine des Cèdres","Caves de la Béroche","Domaine Saint-Sébaste"],"nerd_note":"Oeil de Perdrix is a protected name in Neuchâtel for Pinot Noir rosé.","rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-nidwalden":{"name":"Nidwalden","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Nidwalden has a few small vineyards on the slopes above Lake Lucerne; with Obwalden and Uri it counts about 7 ha.","terroir":"Moraine and limestone.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-obwalden":{"name":"Obwalden","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Obwalden, a mountain canton of central Switzerland, has a handful of hectares of vines; together with Nidwalden and Uri it counts about 7 ha.","terroir":"Moraine slopes near Lake Sarnen.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-schaffhausen":{"name":"Schaffhausen","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC Schaffhausen grows mostly Pinot Noir on limestone and molasse near the Rhine Falls; Hallau is the largest wine commune of German-speaking Switzerland.","producers":["Weingut Pircher","GVS Schachenmann","Weingut Baumann"],"rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-schwyz":{"name":"Schwyz","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":37,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Räuschling, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Schwyz has about 37 ha of vines, mostly at Leutschen near Freienbach on Lake Zurich and on the island of Ufenau, vineyards owned for centuries by the Benedictine abbey of Einsiedeln.","terroir":"Molasse and moraine slopes above Lake Zurich.","producers":["Kloster Einsiedeln"],"nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-solothurn":{"name":"Solothurn","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":13,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Solothurn has about 13 ha of vines, scattered on the foot of the Jura near Solothurn and in the Dorneck district.","terroir":"Jura limestone.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-st-gallen":{"name":"St. Gallen","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":217,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC St. Gallen, mostly in the Rhine valley near the Austrian border, grows Pinot Noir in the warm föhn corridor.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-thunersee":{"name":"Thunersee","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Region","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau, Chasselas","summary":"Thunersee is a small regional AOC within the canton of Bern for the vineyards around Lake Thun, notably at Spiez, below its castle, and Oberhofen. Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and Chasselas grow here within sight of the Bernese Alps.","terroir":"Moraine and limestone slopes on the lake shore, warmed by the lake and the föhn wind.","nerd_note":"Spiez's castle vineyard on the lake shore is the best-known wine site of the Bernese Oberland.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-thurgau":{"name":"Thurgau","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":250,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC Thurgau, home canton of Hermann Müller, grows Müller-Thurgau and Pinot Noir on about 250 hectares along the Thur and Lake Constance.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-ticino":{"name":"Ticino","cat":"AOC","region":"Ticino","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":1028,"styles":["red","white","rose"],"grapes":"Merlot (about 80%), Cabernet Sauvignon, Bondola, Chardonnay","summary":"AOC Ticino covers about 1,030 hectares in the Italian-speaking canton, split by Monte Ceneri into the Sopraceneri (granite, around Bellinzona, Locarno and the Gudo and Giubiasco slopes) and the Sottoceneri (limestone and clay, around Lugano and Mendrisio). Merlot, planted since 1906, is the defining grape, from fresh, cherry-scented reds to Bordeaux-like reserve wines, plus white Merlot. The VITI label marks wines approved by a tasting panel.","terroir":"Granite and gneiss north of Monte Ceneri, limestone and moraine south; humid, sunny, sub-Mediterranean.","producers":["Gialdi","Guido Brivio","Tamborini","Castello di Morcote","Kopp von der Crone Visini","Christian Zündel","Agriloro"],"nerd_note":"Bondola, a local red grape that survived phylloxera, is still made by a few growers as a curiosity.","rslug":"ticino"},"ch-uri":{"name":"Uri","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Uri, the canton of the Gotthard pass, has a few vineyards near Flüelen and Bürglen at the southern end of Lake Lucerne, warmed by the föhn; with Obwalden and Nidwalden it counts about 7 ha.","terroir":"Limestone scree and moraine.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-valais":{"name":"Valais","cat":"AOC","region":"Valais","created":"1990 (cantonal quality hierarchy); AOC rules updated since","area_ha":4600,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Chasselas (Fendant), Pinot Noir, Gamay, Petite Arvine, Syrah, Cornalin, Humagne Rouge, Amigne, Heida and many others","summary":"The cantonal AOC of the Valais covers about 4,600 hectares along the upper Rhône and is the most varied appellation in Switzerland, with dozens of permitted varieties. It contains famous communal names (Fully, Chamoson, Vétroz, Conthey, Sion, Saint-Léonard, Sierre, Salgesch, Leuk, Visperterminen) and Grand Cru rules defined commune by commune. Fendant (Chasselas) and Dôle (a blend in which Pinot Noir dominates, with Gamay) are the classics, but Petite Arvine, Cornalin, Humagne Rouge, Amigne and Heida have made the canton internationally interesting. Chamoson is the largest wine commune of the Valais and a Johannisberg (Sylvaner) stronghold; Fully, on a granite fan, is the capital of Petite Arvine and Gamay; Salgesch, at the language border, is known for Pinot Noir.","terroir":"Schist, gneiss, limestone and alluvial fans on steep slopes in a dry, sunny valley.","producers":["Marie-Thérèse Chappaz","Domaine Jean-René Germanier","Simon Maye et Fils","Denis Mercier","Domaine Cornulus","Provins","Domaine des Muses","Chanton","Robert Gilliard","Domaine Gérald Besse","Albert Mathier"],"nerd_note":"The Valais has a bee-shaped sweetness scale for Amigne de Vétroz: one bee is dry, three bees sweet.","rslug":"valais"},"ch-vaud":{"name":"Vaud","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"Vaud","area_ha":3747,"styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Gamay, Pinot Noir, Gamaret","summary":"Vaud is Switzerland's second wine canton after Valais, with 3,747 ha of vines in 2024, and the spiritual home of Chasselas. Its vineyards run in a long arc along Lake Geneva, from La Côte west of Lausanne through the terraced Lavaux to the Chablais in the Rhône valley, and on to the Côtes de l'Orbe, Bonvillars and the Vaud side of the Vully. Chasselas here is delicate, low in acidity and deeply expressive of soil, from the flinty wines of Yvorne and Aigle to the rich, honeyed whites of Dézaley and Calamin, the canton's two grand cru appellations. Reds from Gamay, Pinot Noir and Gamaret are on the rise.","terroir":"Glacial moraine, limestone scree and conglomerate on south-facing slopes above the lake; the water mirrors light and moderates temperature, and the terraces of Lavaux store the heat of 'three suns': the sky, the lake and the stone walls.","producers":["Louis Bovard","Blaise Duboux","Henri Badoux","Domaine de la Colombe","Bovy"],"nerd_note":"Lavaux's 830 ha of terraces, built by Cistercian monks from the twelfth century, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2007.","rslug":"vaud"},"ch-vully":{"name":"Vully","cat":"AOC","region":"Three Lakes","created":"AOC (intercantonal)","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Chasselas, Pinot Noir, Gamay, Gamaret","summary":"Vully is the vineyard on the slopes of Mont Vully between Lake Murten and Lake Neuchâtel, shared by Vaud and Fribourg, and part of the Three Lakes region. Chasselas, Pinot Noir and the aromatic Traminer are the main wines.","terroir":"Molasse sandstone and moraine.","nerd_note":"Vully is one of the few Swiss AOCs that crosses a cantonal border.","rslug":"three-lakes"},"ch-zug":{"name":"Zug","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Canton","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","area_ha":9,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Zug has about 9 ha of vines, mainly at Walchwil on the sunny eastern shore of Lake Zug.","terroir":"Moraine and molasse.","nerd_note":"Swiss wine law delegates appellations to the cantons, so every wine canton has its own cantonal AOC with its own rules.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-zurich":{"name":"Zürich","cat":"AOC","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","created":"AOC (cantonal)","area_ha":644,"styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau and others","summary":"AOC Zürich covers about 640 hectares on the shores of Lake Zurich and in the Zürcher Weinland near Schaffhausen. Pinot Noir (Blauburgunder), Müller-Thurgau and the old local white Räuschling dominate.","producers":["Schwarzenbach","Zahner","Weingut Rütihof"],"rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"},"ch-zurichsee":{"name":"Zürichsee","cat":"AOC","subcat":"Region","region":"German-speaking Switzerland","subregion":"German-speaking Switzerland","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Räuschling, Pinot Noir, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Zürichsee is the regional AOC for the vineyards along both shores of Lake Zurich, from Stäfa, Meilen and Herrliberg to Wädenswil. The local speciality is Räuschling, an old white grape now rare elsewhere, alongside Pinot Noir and Müller-Thurgau. Canton Zurich as a whole has 618 ha of vines.","terroir":"Molasse and moraine slopes above the lake, which moderates the climate.","nerd_note":"Müller-Thurgau is named after Hermann Müller from Thurgau, who bred it in Geisenheim in 1882 and later led the research station at Wädenswil on this lake.","rslug":"german-speaking-switzerland"}}};
